<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amda Seyon I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">acceded <a href="/years/1314">1314</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Amda Seyon · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amda Seyon I was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> who acceded in 1314. He conducted successful campaigns against the Muslim states to the east and south, greatly expanding the power and territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a>. His reign is regarded as a key stage in the foundation and military consolidation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i.jpg" alt="Amda Seyon I" width="303" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amda_Seyon1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amda Seyon, whose name means Pillar of Zion, took the throne name Gebre Mesqel, Servant of the Cross, and ruled until 1344. How he became emperor is not known, though he may have been drawn into a succession struggle against Wedem Arad, whose son he probably was. When monks led by Basalota Mika'el accused him of incest for marrying Wedem Arad's concubine Jan Mogassa, he claimed to be the son of the emperor's brother Qedma Asgad, beat one accuser and exiled the others to Dembiya and Begemder.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>A note found by Taddesse Tamrat in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the monastery of Lake Hayq records his conquest of pagan Damot and the Muslim Hadiya Kingdom in 1316/7, whose peoples he deported. He then turned north against Enderta, whose governors had grown nearly independent; after crushing a revolt he placed a military colony at Amba Senayata and set his queen Bilén Saba, then his son Bahr Seged, over the province. In 1329 he campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semien">Semien</a>, Wegera, Tselemt and Tsegede, where the Beta Israel had been gaining ground.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> an-Nasir Muhammad persecuted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Amda Seyon sent envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1321-2 threatening reprisals and the diversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. Sultan Haqq ad-Din I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a> seized and killed one envoy, Ti'yintay, and the emperor invaded Ifat, sacked its capital and pillaged its provinces. Its account, The Glorious Victories, tells how he defeated the rising of Sabr ad-Din, who aimed to rule all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, and pressed on to Adal. Most scholars date that campaign to 1332, though G.W.B. Huntingford, after James Bruce, preferred 1329. Bertrand Hirsch has proposed that the chronicle is a 15th-century epic, which others reject.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His conquests expanded Christian territory for centuries, spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to frontier lands and legitimised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a>. The historian Mohammed Hassen wrote that his wars created an empire but not a nation, bringing enrichment to Christians and destruction to Muslims. The empire had almost no administration and no fixed capital, and was held together by military force, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chewa">Chewa</a> regiments and land grants to officers. In his reign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast</a> is said to have been translated from Arabic, and the Soldiers Songs are the earliest surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a>. Around 1350 he appeared as Abdeselib in the Spanish Book of Knowledge of All Kingdoms, the first Ethiopian emperor known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amda Seyon I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Consolidation came under <b>Amda Seyon I</b>, who acceded in 1314, organised the army into standing chewa regiments, and expanded southward across the Awash River and eastward, subduing Ifat, Dawaro, Bale, and other Muslim principalities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When Emperor <b>Amda Seyon</b> threatened in 1320 to divert the Nile and starve Egypt unless the Mamluk sultan al-Nasir Muhammad stopped persecuting the Copts, the Egyptians dismissed it as an idle threat; the fear that Ethiopia might tamper with the Nile nonetheless stayed with them for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-ifat">Sultanate of Ifat · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…“Glory of the Kings”, by the Nebura'ed Yeshaq of Aksum, retold the story of the Queen of Sheba, Solomon and Menelik I; the first contemporary royal chronicles date to <b>Amda Seyon I</b> (1314–1344), and around 200 texts on Ethiopian saints were written between the 14th and 19th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/language/geez">Ge'ez · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Al-Umari presents it as a small agricultural state paying tribute to the Ethiopian Empire after the campaigns of <b>Amda Seyon</b> in the 1330s.</blockquote><a href="/state/arababni">Arababni</a></li><li><blockquote>Like its neighbours it paid tribute to the Ethiopian Empire after the campaigns of <b>Amda Seyon</b> in the 1330s.</blockquote><a href="/state/dara-sultanate">Dara Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>It appears in the 13th century and became a tributary of the Ethiopian Empire under <b>Amda Seyon</b> in the 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/fatagar-sultanate">Fatagar Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
